---
title: How Many Jobs Should You Apply To Per Week?
publishedAt: 2026-11-27
type: job-seekers
description: >-
  There's no magic number. Set a weekly job application goal from the hours you
  have and the time each good application takes, then adjust it from replies.
---
**Quick answer:** There's no single right number. Work it out from your own week: divide the hours you can give to applying by the time a good application takes you. For many people that means a few a week while employed and more when searching full time. Pick a goal you can keep for a month, count only applications you actually sent, and change the goal or your approach based on how many companies reply.

Advice like "send 100 applications a week" sounds productive. In practice it often leads to untailored applications, then burnout. A steady pace you can keep is more useful.

## How do you calculate a weekly application goal?

**Weekly goal = hours available for applying ÷ hours per application**

Applying isn't your whole job search. Keep time aside for finding roles, following up, networking and interview practice.

Time yourself on your next three applications, including reading the posting, tailoring your resume and answering questions. Then use your own figure. The table below shows how the sum works with example numbers.

{% table %}
-
- Hours a week for applying
- At 30 minutes each
- At 1 hour each
- At 2 hours each
---
- 3
- 6
- 3
- 1
---
- 6
- 12
- 6
- 3
---
- 15
- 30
- 15
- 7
{% /table %}

## Is it better to send more applications or better ones?

Mix them deliberately. Sort roles into two groups:

- **Priority roles:** strong fit, company you want. Tailor your resume, write thoughtful answers, and look for a referral or a direct contact.
- **Good-enough roles:** a reasonable fit. Use your best resume for that role type and apply quickly.

If you can't tell which group a role belongs in, compare its must-have requirements with your resume. Missing most of them usually means your time is better spent elsewhere.

## How do you set a weekly goal on GetHired?

1. Open your [dashboard](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/dashboard).
2. In **Your pipeline**, choose **Set a weekly goal**.
3. Pick 3, 5 or 10, or enter any whole number from 1 to 100.

The dashboard then shows your applications **in the last 7 days** against your goal. It's a rolling window, so it doesn't reset on Monday. You can edit or remove the goal at any time.

It counts applications on your [tracker](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/job-application-tracker-how-to-keep-track-of-every-application), so add the ones you send on other sites.

## How do you know if your number is working?

Count replies as well as applications. Your GetHired pipeline shows how many applications are in each stage, and once you have at least three, the share that got a reply. A rejection counts as a reply. An application you withdrew or marked as ghosted doesn't.

Treat early percentages carefully. With only a handful of applications, one reply moves the figure a lot. Wait until you have a few weeks of data.

{% table %}
-
- What you see
- Likely problem
- What to try
---
- Hitting your goal, very few replies
- Targeting or resume
- Apply to closer matches and [tailor your resume](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-tailor-your-resume-for-each-job-without-making-things-up)
---
- Replies, but few interviews
- Screening answers or fit
- Review your answers and salary expectations
---
- Interviews, no offers
- Interview performance
- Move time from applying to [mock interviews](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-practise-for-interviews-with-an-ai-mock-interview)
---
- Missing your goal often
- Goal too high, or too few suitable roles
- Lower it, or widen one filter in your search
{% /table %}

## How do you keep a steady pace without burning out?

- **Batch the work.** Find and save roles in one session, and apply in another.
- **Let roles come to you.** [Job alerts](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-set-up-job-alerts-that-only-send-jobs-worth-applying-to) email you new matches, so you spend less time searching.
- **Keep a saved list.** When you sit down to apply, you already know where to start. The dashboard shows saved jobs you haven't applied to.
- **Handle follow-ups first.** An application already in motion is worth more attention than a new one.
- **Take days off.** A weekly goal leaves room for them in a way a daily target doesn't.

## Frequently asked questions

### Is applying to 5 jobs a week enough?

It can be, especially if you're employed and each application is well targeted. Judge it by replies over several weeks, and raise it if you have time and suitable roles.

### Is applying to 50 jobs a day a good idea?

It's hard to read postings properly or tailor anything at that pace, and you can easily lose track of where you applied. Most people get more from fewer, better matched applications.

### Should I apply if I don't meet every requirement?

If you meet the core requirements and miss a few preferences, it's usually worth applying. If you miss a stated must-have, such as a required licence or the right to work, put your time elsewhere.

### Do saved jobs count towards my weekly goal?

No. Only applications count.

[Set your weekly goal on your dashboard](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/dashboard) and review it after a month.
